  "id": "833096530",
  "subject": [
    "ANE"
  ],
  "title": "Dur-Sharrukku/Sippar-Aruru",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/unlocated",
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/settlement"
  ],
  "details": "<p><em>The Helsinki Atlas of the Near East</em> in the Neo-Assyrian Period tentatively proposes that Sippar-Aruru is to be identified\u00a0as modern Tell ed-Der, a site generally regarded by scholars as ancient Sippar-Amnanum (also called Sippar-Anun\u012btu). There is presently no (textual or archaeological)\u00a0evidence to support Parpola's proposed identification of\u00a0Dur-Sharrukku/Sippar-Aruru. See\u00a0<a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://pleiades.stoa.org/places/392373882\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">Sippar-Amnanum</a> in Pleiades.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Dur-Sharrukku is an unlocated city in northern Babylonia that also goes by the name Sippar-Aruru (starting in the 7th century B.C.). In the 8th century B.C., Dur-Sharrukku served as an Assyrian provincial center. ",
